  "prompt_text": "The figure is a conceptual overview of the many open questions surrounding Antarctic krill under environmental change. A large central swarm of orange krill fills a blue ocean-shaped panel, while ships, nets, whales, birds, and sea ice suggest the broader ecosystem and fishery context. Around the swarm, small illustrated insets highlight key themes: individual krill behavior, reproduction, biomass estimation, acoustic or net sampling, predator interactions, fishing vessels, and geographic redistribution around Antarctica.\n\nTaken together, the figure emphasizes that krill are being studied not just as a population total, but as changing swarms with different sizes, densities, spacing, and vertical positions in the water column. It also points to uncertainty about whether krill will remain in dense surface aggregations or become more dispersed, deeper, or shifted poleward and around the continent. Several questions connect these changes to practical consequences: how to monitor krill efficiently, how fisheries may be affected, and how predators’ access to krill may change.\n\nOverall, the visual presents krill as a pivotal species whose future depends on linked physical, biological, and observational processes, with major implications for Antarctic ecosystems and resource management.",
